{
  "$schema": "https://unpkg.com/knip@6/schema-jsonc.json",

  // Knip finds unused FILES, unused EXPORTS and unused DEPENDENCIES — the
  // project-wide layer. The per-file layer (unused locals and parameters) is
  // already covered by tsconfig.base.json's `noUnusedLocals` /
  // `noUnusedParameters`, so the two do not overlap. See
  // docs/project/static-analysis.md.
  //
  // Deliberately NOT configured here:
  //   - `paths` for the `@/*` alias. Knip reads that alias out of vite.config.ts
  //     and vitest.config.ts via its own vite/vitest plugins, not out of any
  //     tsconfig, so declaring it here would be dead config that looks
  //     load-bearing. Verified: with no `paths` at all, `@/components/ui/button`
  //     resolves and only the genuinely-unused `collapsible.tsx` is flagged.
  //   - workspaces. Those key on package.json locations and this repo has one;
  //     src/web cannot be a workspace without a package.json of its own.

  "entry": [
    // The pipeline stages (`tsx src/fetch.ts` and friends) are NOT listed here.
    // Knip reads them straight out of package.json's scripts, and listing them
    // again is redundant — it says so itself, as a "Remove redundant entry
    // pattern" hint. Adding a stage to package.json is therefore all it takes.

    // The Vercel deploy, reachable only through vercel.json and so invisible to
    // every other tool here. Whatever is in api/ is what Vercel routes to.
    //
    // Deliberately `{ts,js}` and a glob rather than a filename, because this
    // corner is mid-rewrite by someone else and has changed shape twice in one
    // afternoon: `api/[...path].ts` → `api/[...path].js` → `api/index.js`, with
    // the real handler moving to src/vercel.ts and vite.api.config.ts building
    // it. (The forwarder is JavaScript on purpose: Vercel's Node builder cannot
    // drive this repo's TypeScript 7 and ships a broken function while
    // reporting success — the file's own header has the account.)
    //
    // The first version of this config named those files exactly, which meant it
    // described a working tree rather than the repo: on a clean checkout it
    // pointed at files that were not there and missed the ones that were. A glob
    // is right in both states, which is what a config in a tree several agents
    // are editing has to be.
    "api/**/*.{ts,js}",
    "src/vercel*.ts",
    "vite.api.config.ts",

    // Operational scripts and evals, each behind its own npm script.
    "scripts/*.ts",
    "evals/*.ts"

    // Not listed on purpose: vite.config.ts, vitest.config.ts,
    // drizzle.config.ts, index.html -> src/web/main.tsx, and tests/**. Knip's
    // vite / vitest / drizzle plugins discover all of those on their own.
  ],

  // The CSS is in here on purpose, and it is load-bearing: the only reference
  // to @fontsource-variable/geist{,-mono} anywhere in the repo is an `@import`
  // in src/web/tailwind.css, and the only reference to `tailwindcss` itself is
  // two more. Leave CSS out and Knip reports all three as unused dependencies —
  // three false positives that would have been "fixed" by uninstalling the
  // fonts the reading view is set in.
  //
  // The root-level globs are here for the same reason in reverse: without them
  // a stray `scratch-*.mts` at the repo root is not in the project at all, so it
  // is never reported as unused. A file that falls out of scope looks exactly
  // like a file with nothing wrong with it. This earned its keep: it is how
  // `scratch-redact.mts` stayed visible until it was deleted on 2026-08-28,
  // three days after the function it was probing had shipped. `scratch-tok2.mts`
  // is still here and still reported, and that is the right answer for it — see
  // docs/plans/simplification-wave-2.md.
  "project": [
    "src/**/*.{ts,tsx,css}",
    "styles/**/*.css",
    "api/**/*.{ts,js}",
    "scripts/**/*.ts",
    "evals/**/*.ts",
    "*.{ts,mts,cts}"
  ],

  // Binaries invoked from npm scripts and tests that are installed globally
  // rather than as dependencies. Both are genuinely expected to be on PATH:
  // `supabase` is the CLI from docs/project/supabase-local.md, and `openssl` is
  // used by tests/db-tls.test.ts to inspect the remote's certificate.
  "ignoreBinaries": ["supabase", "openssl"],

  // playwright-core is loaded by scripts/remote-smoke-browser.mjs through
  // createRequire() against a path computed at runtime, because that script
  // runs on the remote box and has to find whichever copy is there. Knip
  // resolves static imports, so it cannot see that and reports the dependency
  // as unused. It is not: dropping it puts the box back on borrowing a
  // playwright-core from the npx cache at whatever version the MCPs bundled.
  // See docs/project/browser-control.md.
  "ignoreDependencies": ["playwright-core"]
}
